Divide the microseconds of the Java time as a 32-bit value.

suseconds_t is 64 bits wide with the time64 musl ABI, and ARM divides a
64-bit value only in a library routine, which took most of the instructions
of System.currentTimeMillis(). The microseconds are below one million, so the
32-bit division gives the same milliseconds.

--- a/cldc/src/vm/os/linux/OS_linux.cpp
+++ b/cldc/src/vm/os/linux/OS_linux.cpp
@@ -146,8 +146,9 @@
 jlong Os::java_time_millis() {
     struct timeval tv;
     ::jvm_gettimeofday(&tv, NULL);
-    /* We adjust to 1000 ticks per second */
-    return (jlong)tv.tv_sec * 1000 + tv.tv_usec/1000;
+    /* We adjust to 1000 ticks per second. tv_usec is below one million but
+       may be a 64-bit type, which ARM can only divide in software. */
+    return (jlong)tv.tv_sec * 1000 + (jint)tv.tv_usec / 1000;
 }

 #if SUPPORTS_MONOTONIC_CLOCK
